<section id="Ax1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix">Appendix</h2>

<div id="Ax1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this appendix, we provide more implementation details, experiments, analysis, and discussions for a comprehensive evaluation and understanding of FE2E. Detailed contents are listed as follows:</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Experiment Settings</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Auxiliary Dispersion Loss</h3>

<figure id="A1.T5" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center ltx_align_top ltx_align_floatright" style="width:198.7pt;">
<br class="ltx_break ltx_break">
<div id="A1.T5.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:198.7pt;height:37pt;vertical-align:-16.6pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-32.8pt,6.1pt) scale(0.752099300666189,0.752099300666189) ;">
<table id="A1.T5.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A1.T5.1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A1.T5.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;" rowspan="2"><span id="A1.T5.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Method</span></th>
<th id="A1.T5.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;" colspan="2">KITTI</th>
<th id="A1.T5.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;" colspan="2">ETH3D</th></tr>
<tr id="A1.T5.1.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A1.T5.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;">AbsRel<math id="A1.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="A1.T5.1.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;"><math id="A1.SS1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta 1\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta 1\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="A1.T5.1.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;">AbsRel<math id="A1.SS1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="A1.T5.1.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;"><math id="A1.SS1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta 1\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta 1\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A1.T5.1.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A1.T5.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;">Baseline (CV + FS)</th>
<td id="A1.T5.1.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;">8.6</td>
<td id="A1.T5.1.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;">94.0</td>
<td id="A1.T5.1.1.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;">4.8</td>
<td id="A1.T5.1.1.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;">97.3</td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T5.1.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A1.T5.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;">+ Disperse Loss (DL)</th>
<td id="A1.T5.1.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;">8.4</td>
<td id="A1.T5.1.1.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;">94.4</td>
<td id="A1.T5.1.1.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;">4.5</td>
<td id="A1.T5.1.1.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;">97.6</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Table 5: </span>Ablation study on Disperse Loss. The baseline is the ID4 model in main paper, Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.4 Ablation Study ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ From Editor to Dense Geometry Estimator" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following Diffuse-and-Disperse <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang &amp; He, 2025</a>)</cite>, we apply this loss to the output of the 9th block:</p>
<table id="A1.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{disp}}=\log\mathbb{E}_{i,j}\left[\exp(-\|\eta_{i}-\eta_{j}\|_{2}^{2}/\tau)\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>disp</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em">/</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{disp}}=\log\mathbb{E}_{i,j}\left[\exp(-\|\eta_{i}-\eta_{j}\|_{2}^{2}/\tau)\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> are the output features for the <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th and <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th samples in a batch, respectively, and temperature <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, the training loss is defined as: <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{train}=\mathcal{L}_{fm}+\lambda\mathcal{L}_{\text{disp}},\lambda=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>disp</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{train}=\mathcal{L}_{fm}+\lambda\mathcal{L}_{\text{disp}},\lambda=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>. The choices of <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, and block all follow the optimal hyperparameters identified in the experiments from Diffuse-and-Disperse.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For integrity, we also conducted ablation studies on this dispersed loss. The performance gains observed in Table <a href="#A1.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ A.1 Auxiliary Dispersion Loss ‣ Appendix A Experiment Settings ‣ From Editor to Dense Geometry Estimator" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> confirm that this loss is also effective for dense geometric estimation tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Evaluation Datasets</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate our model on two tasks: <span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Zero-shot Affine-Invariant Depth Estimation.</span> We evaluate on five standard benchmarks: NYUv2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Silberman et al., 2012</a>)</cite>, ScanNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al., 2017</a>)</cite>, KITTI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geiger et al., 2012</a>)</cite>, ETH3D <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schops et al., 2017</a>)</cite>, and DIODE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vasiljevic et al., 2019</a>)</cite>. Following standard practice, we report the Absolute Relative error (AbsRel) and <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> accuracy. <span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Surface Normal Prediction.</span> We evaluate on NYUv2, ScanNet, iBims-1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Koch et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, and Sintel <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Butler et al., 2012</a>)</cite> benchmarks. The evaluation metrics are the mean angular error (MeanErr) and the percentage of pixels with an angular error below <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11.25^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>11.25</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11.25^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Evaluation Metrics</h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For zero-shot depth estimation, similar to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ke et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, we employ the following evaluation metrics:</p>
<ul id="A1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">AbsRel: <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ \frac{1}{|{M}_{vl}|}\sum_{d\in{M}_{vl}}\left|d-d_{gt}\right|/d_{gt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ \frac{1}{|{M}_{vl}|}\sum_{d\in{M}_{vl}}\left|d-d_{gt}\right|/d_{gt}</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{a}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{a}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>: percentage of <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max(\frac{d}{d_{gt}},\frac{d_{gt}}{d})&lt;1.25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1.25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max(\frac{d}{d_{gt}},\frac{d_{gt}}{d})&lt;1.25</annotation></semantics></math> ;</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{gt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{gt}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> denote the GT and estimated pixel depth, <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{M}_{vl}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{M}_{vl}</annotation></semantics></math> is the valid mask (mask rules are consistent with <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2024</a>)</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For zero-shot normal estimation, we use the following evaluation metrics:</p>
<ul id="A1.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A1.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{MeanErr}=\frac{1}{|{M}_{vl}|}\sum_{\mathbf{n}\in{M}_{vl}}\frac{180}{\pi}\arccos(\text{clamp}(\mathbf{n}\cdot\mathbf{n}_{gt},-1,1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>MeanErr</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>𝐧</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mfrac><mn>180</mn><mi>π</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>arccos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>clamp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐧</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>𝐧</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{MeanErr}=\frac{1}{|{M}_{vl}|}\sum_{\mathbf{n}\in{M}_{vl}}\frac{180}{\pi}\arccos(\text{clamp}(\mathbf{n}\cdot\mathbf{n}_{gt},-1,1))</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11.25^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>11.25</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11.25^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math>: The percentage of <math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐧</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{n}</annotation></semantics></math> where the angular error is less than <math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11.25^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>11.25</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11.25^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A1.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{n}_{gt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐧</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{n}_{gt}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐧</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{n}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the GT and estimated normal vector.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Training Details of Finetune Analysis</h2>

<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Improved Experiment Setup</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For clarity, we term the direct adaptation of the original editing/generative formulation as <span id="A2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“DirectAdapt”</span> (Sec <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 From Editor To Estimator ‣ 3 Methods ‣ From Editor to Dense Geometry Estimator" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>), and Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.4 Ablation Study ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ From Editor to Dense Geometry Estimator" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows that <span id="A2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">DirectAdapt</span> fails to achieve satisfactory performance. To address this, we introduce two key improvements on training objective (Sec <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Consistent Velocity Flow Matching with Deterministic Departure ‣ 3 Methods ‣ From Editor to Dense Geometry Estimator" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>) and GT quantization(Sec <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Logarithmic Annotation Quantization ‣ 3 Methods ‣ From Editor to Dense Geometry Estimator" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>). They can benefit both editing and generative models, and these <span id="A2.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">improved</span> models are better for analyzed our core motivation (Sec <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Fine-tuning Analysis of Editor and Generator ‣ 3 Methods ‣ From Editor to Dense Geometry Estimator" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>), as they isolate the error from training data and the denoising process. We finally introduce joint training on the editing-based model to obtain <span id="A2.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FE2E</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Implementation Details of Generative-based Models</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Step1X-Edit is fine-tuned from the generative model FLUX, and both share an almost identical DiT architecture. To further reduce confounding factors, we follow the Step1X-Edit protocol and replace the original FLUX input with a horizontally concatenated noise and RGB image. All hyperparameters, including LoRA settings, optimizer, and training data, are kept exactly the same as those used for FE2E in-depth estimation.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">FLUX consists of 38 block layers, each producing outputs of consistent dimensions. After rearrangement, the feature map has the shape <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\times 192\times H/8\times W/8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>192</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\times 192\times H/8\times W/8</annotation></semantics></math>, where B is the batch size, H and W are the height and width of the input image. Typically, the output from the final block is projected to 16 channels and passed to the VAE for reconstruction to <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\times 3\times H\times W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\times 3\times H\times W</annotation></semantics></math>. For visualization, we chose 1, 20, and 35 blocks, operate on the <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\times 192\times H/8\times W/8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>192</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\times 192\times H/8\times W/8</annotation></semantics></math> feature map, normalize it to <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\times 1\times H/8\times W/8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\times 1\times H/8\times W/8</annotation></semantics></math> using the L2 norm, upsample it to <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\times 1\times H\times W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\times 1\times H\times W</annotation></semantics></math>, and finally visualize it using the Rainbow colormap. The visualization of depth and normals follows the approach of Lotus.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Since our experimental comparisons are conducted using the <span id="A2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">improved</span> model, only one single “denoising” step is performed during inference. Consequently, the output from the VAE decoder directly represents the depth map (the <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\times 3\times H\times W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\times 3\times H\times W</annotation></semantics></math> output mentioned before was averaged to obtain a 1-channel depth map), which makes it easier to visualize meaningful features.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Quantization Error Calculation Details</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The following calculations are based on the effective depth range of 0-80m from the Virtual KITTI dataset. The normalization scheme consistently maps an input domain <math id="A3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> to the VAE’s mandatory input range of [-1, 1] using the standard min-max scaling formula: <math id="A3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=2\times\frac{X-X_{min}}{X_{max}-X_{min}}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=2\times\frac{X-X_{min}}{X_{max}-X_{min}}-1</annotation></semantics></math>. While other mapping schemes from [0m, 80m] to [-1, 1] may exist, they are not explored in this work. All calculations use the worst-case precision of BF16 over the [-1, 1] interval, which corresponds to a single quantization step of <math id="A3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta V\approx 1/256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta V\approx 1/256</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Uniform Quantization</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this scheme, the depth value <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is linearly mapped to the [-1, 1] interval. The depth range is <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[D_{min},D_{max}]=[0\text{m},80\text{m}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>80</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[D_{min},D_{max}]=[0\text{m},80\text{m}]</annotation></semantics></math>.
The mapping function is <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=2\times\frac{D-0}{80-0}-1=\frac{D}{40}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>80</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>D</mi><mn>40</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=2\times\frac{D-0}{80-0}-1=\frac{D}{40}-1</annotation></semantics></math>.
A quantization step of <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta V=1/256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta V=1/256</annotation></semantics></math> in the normalized space corresponds to an error <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta D</annotation></semantics></math> in the real-world depth space. This error is constant across the entire depth range:</p>
<table id="A3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta D=40\times\Delta V=40\times\frac{1}{256}\approx 0.15625" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>40</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>40</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>256</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.15625</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta D=40\times\Delta V=40\times\frac{1}{256}\approx 0.15625</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">At 80m:
Error <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx{16cm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx{16cm}</annotation></semantics></math>.
AbsRel = <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{0.16\text{m}}{80\text{m}}={0.002}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>0.16</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mn>80</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>0.002</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{0.16\text{m}}{80\text{m}}={0.002}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">At 0.1m:
Error <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx{16cm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx{16cm}</annotation></semantics></math>.
AbsRel = <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{0.16\text{m}}{0.1\text{m}}={1.600}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>0.16</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>1.600</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{0.16\text{m}}{0.1\text{m}}={1.600}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This method yields an unacceptably large relative error at close distances.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Inverse Quantization</h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This scheme quantizes the reciprocal of depth, i.e., disparity <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=1/D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=1/D</annotation></semantics></math>. We consider an effective depth range of <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0.1\text{m},80\text{m}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>80</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0.1\text{m},80\text{m}]</annotation></semantics></math> to avoid division by zero.
The corresponding disparity range is <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[P_{min},P_{max}]=[1/80,1/0.1]=[0.0125,10]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>80</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0.0125</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[P_{min},P_{max}]=[1/80,1/0.1]=[0.0125,10]</annotation></semantics></math>.
The disparity <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is linearly mapped to [-1, 1]. The quantization step in disparity, <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta P</annotation></semantics></math>, is constant:</p>
<table id="A3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta P=(P_{max}-P_{min})\times\frac{\Delta V}{2}=(10-0.0125)\times\frac{1/256}{2}\approx 0.0195." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>0.0125</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.0195</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta P=(P_{max}-P_{min})\times\frac{\Delta V}{2}=(10-0.0125)\times\frac{1/256}{2}\approx 0.0195.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The relationship between depth error <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta D</annotation></semantics></math> and disparity error <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta P</annotation></semantics></math> is given by <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta D\approx|\frac{d(1/P)}{dP}|\Delta P=\frac{1}{P^{2}}\Delta P=D^{2}\Delta P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta D\approx|\frac{d(1/P)}{dP}|\Delta P=\frac{1}{P^{2}}\Delta P=D^{2}\Delta P</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">At 80m:
Error = <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(80\text{m})^{2}\times 0.0195=6400\times 0.0195\approx 124.8\text{m}\approx{125m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>80</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>0.0195</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>6400</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>0.0195</mn></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>124.8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>125</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(80\text{m})^{2}\times 0.0195=6400\times 0.0195\approx 124.8\text{m}\approx{125m}</annotation></semantics></math>.
AbsRel = <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{125\text{m}}{80\text{m}}\approx{1.563}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>125</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mn>80</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mfrac><mo>≈</mo><mn>1.563</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{125\text{m}}{80\text{m}}\approx{1.563}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">At 0.1m:
Error = <math id="A3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0.1\text{m})^{2}\times 0.0195=0.01\times 0.0195=0.000195\text{m}\approx{0.2mm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>0.0195</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0.01</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>0.0195</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0.000195</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>0.2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0.1\text{m})^{2}\times 0.0195=0.01\times 0.0195=0.000195\text{m}\approx{0.2mm}</annotation></semantics></math>.
AbsRel = <math id="A3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{0.0002\text{m}}{0.1\text{m}}={0.002}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>0.0002</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>0.002</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{0.0002\text{m}}{0.1\text{m}}={0.002}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">As mentioned in the main text, the disparities for 39m and 78m are <math id="A3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/39\approx 0.0256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>39</mn></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.0256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/39\approx 0.0256</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/78\approx 0.0128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>78</mn></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.0128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/78\approx 0.0128</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. Their difference is <math id="A3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 0.0128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.0128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 0.0128</annotation></semantics></math>, which is smaller than the disparity quantization step <math id="A3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta P\approx 0.0195" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.0195</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta P\approx 0.0195</annotation></semantics></math>, making them indistinguishable after quantization. This scheme fails completely at large distances.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.3 </span>Logarithmic Quantization</h3>

<div id="A3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This scheme quantizes the logarithmic depth, <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{log}=\ln(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{log}=\ln(D)</annotation></semantics></math>. We again consider the depth range <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0.1\text{m},80\text{m}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>80</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0.1\text{m},80\text{m}]</annotation></semantics></math>.
The corresponding log-depth range is <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\ln(0.1),\ln(80)]\approx[-2.30,4.38]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>80</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2.30</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>4.38</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\ln(0.1),\ln(80)]\approx[-2.30,4.38]</annotation></semantics></math>.
The log-depth <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{log}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{log}</annotation></semantics></math> is linearly mapped to [-1, 1]. The quantization step in log-depth, <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta D_{log}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta D_{log}</annotation></semantics></math>, is constant:</p>
<table id="A3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta D_{log}=(\ln(80)-\ln(0.1))\times\frac{\Delta V}{2}=(4.38-(-2.30))\times\frac{1/256}{2}\approx 0.013." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>80</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4.38</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2.30</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.013</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta D_{log}=(\ln(80)-\ln(0.1))\times\frac{\Delta V}{2}=(4.38-(-2.30))\times\frac{1/256}{2}\approx 0.013.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The relationship between depth error <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta D</annotation></semantics></math> and log-depth error <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta D_{log}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta D_{log}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta D\approx|\frac{d(e^{D_{log}})}{dD_{log}}|\Delta D_{log}=e^{D_{log}}\Delta D_{log}=D\cdot\Delta D_{log}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta D\approx|\frac{d(e^{D_{log}})}{dD_{log}}|\Delta D_{log}=e^{D_{log}}\Delta D_{log}=D\cdot\Delta D_{log}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This implies that the absolute relative error, AbsRel = <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta D/D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta D/D</annotation></semantics></math>, is approximately constant and equal to <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta D_{log}\approx 0.013" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.013</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta D_{log}\approx 0.013</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">At 80m:
AbsRel <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx{0.013}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.013</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx{0.013}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Error = <math id="A3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="80\text{m}\times 0.013=1.04\text{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>80</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>0.013</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1.04</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">80\text{m}\times 0.013=1.04\text{m}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">At 0.1m:
AbsRel <math id="A3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx{0.013}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.013</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx{0.013}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Error = <math id="A3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.1\text{m}\times 0.013=0.0013\text{m}={1.3mm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>0.013</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0.0013</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1.3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1\text{m}\times 0.013=0.0013\text{m}={1.3mm}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This method maintains a reasonable and nearly constant relative error across both near and far ranges, making it a well-balanced and effective solution. The percentile-based normalization used in the main text is a more robust implementation of this fundamental principle.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Preliminaries of Flow Matching</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Flow Matching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lipman et al., 2022</a>)</cite> is a highly effective framework for training Continuous Normalizing Flows (CNFs). The core idea is to smoothly transform a simple prior distribution <math id="A4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., the standard Gaussian distribution <math id="A4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{I})</annotation></semantics></math>) into a complex target data distribution <math id="A4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> over a continuous time variable <math id="A4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This transformation process can be described by an Ordinary Differential Equation (ODE), where the velocity at any time <math id="A4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> and point <math id="A4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by a vector field <math id="A4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{t}(\mathbf{z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{t}(\mathbf{z})</annotation></semantics></math>. However, estimating this marginal vector field <math id="A4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{t}(\mathbf{z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{t}(\mathbf{z})</annotation></semantics></math> directly from data samples is challenging. The Flow Matching framework elegantly bypasses this issue by regressing a much simpler and easier-to-compute conditional vector field <math id="A4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}(\mathbf{z}|\mathbf{z}_{0},\mathbf{z}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(\mathbf{z}|\mathbf{z}_{0},\mathbf{z}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> instead.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, we first sample a pair of points, <math id="A4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{z}_{0},\mathbf{z}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{z}_{0},\mathbf{z}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, from the prior distribution <math id="A4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and the target distribution <math id="A4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. We then define a simple path <math id="A4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="A4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and its corresponding conditional vector field <math id="A4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}=\frac{d\mathbf{z}_{t}}{dt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}=\frac{d\mathbf{z}_{t}}{dt}</annotation></semantics></math>. It has been proven that if a neural network <math id="A4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}(\mathbf{z},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}(\mathbf{z},t)</annotation></semantics></math> is trained to approximate this simple conditional vector field <math id="A4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, then in expectation over all sample pairs <math id="A4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{z}_{0},\mathbf{z}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{z}_{0},\mathbf{z}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and time <math id="A4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the network <math id="A4.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> will converge to the complex marginal vector field <math id="A4.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> that we truly wish to learn.</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Rectified Flow</h4>

<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2022</a>)</cite> presents a particularly simple and powerful instance of Flow Matching. It defines the path between <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> as a straight line:</p>
<table id="A4.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{t}=t\mathbf{z}_{1}+(1-t)\mathbf{z}_{0},\quad t\in[0,1]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{t}=t\mathbf{z}_{1}+(1-t)\mathbf{z}_{0},\quad t\in[0,1].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The derivative of this path is trivial, yielding a constant velocity vector that is independent of both time and space:</p>
<table id="A4.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}=\frac{d\mathbf{z}_{t}}{dt}=\mathbf{z}_{1}-\mathbf{z}_{0}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}=\frac{d\mathbf{z}_{t}}{dt}=\mathbf{z}_{1}-\mathbf{z}_{0}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Consequently, the training objective (loss function) becomes exceedingly simple: aligning the neural network’s prediction with this constant velocity vector <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A4.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\mathbb{E}_{t,\mathbf{z}_{1},\mathbf{z}_{0}}\|(\mathbf{z}_{1}-\mathbf{z}_{0})-f_{\theta}(\mathbf{z}_{t},t)\|^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\mathbb{E}_{t,\mathbf{z}_{1},\mathbf{z}_{0}}\|(\mathbf{z}_{1}-\mathbf{z}_{0})-f_{\theta}(\mathbf{z}_{t},t)\|^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Application in <span id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">DirectAdapt</span></h4>

<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we adapt this framework for a conditional image editing task. Our goal is not to learn an unconditional generative model, but rather a flow from noise <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}^{y}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>y</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}^{y}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to the target geometry latent <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}^{y}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>y</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}^{y}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, guided by the input image <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> (encoded as <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}^{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>x</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}^{x}</annotation></semantics></math>). Therefore, our velocity prediction model <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> must take <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}^{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>x</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}^{x}</annotation></semantics></math> as an additional condition. As shown in Eq. <a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3.2 From Editor To Estimator ‣ 3 Methods ‣ From Editor to Dense Geometry Estimator" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> in the main text, our loss function is:</p>
<table id="A4.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\mathbb{E}_{t,\mathbf{z}^{y}_{1},\mathbf{z}^{y}_{0}}\|(\mathbf{z}^{y}_{1}-\mathbf{z}^{y}_{0})-f_{\theta}(t,\mathbf{z}^{x})\|^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>y</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>y</mi></msubsup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>y</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>y</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>x</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\mathbb{E}_{t,\mathbf{z}^{y}_{1},\mathbf{z}^{y}_{0}}\|(\mathbf{z}^{y}_{1}-\mathbf{z}^{y}_{0})-f_{\theta}(t,\mathbf{z}^{x})\|^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">During inference, we generate the target latent <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{z}}^{y}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><mi>y</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{z}}^{y}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> by solving the following ODE, with <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}^{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>x</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}^{x}</annotation></semantics></math> serving as the guiding condition:</p>
<table id="A4.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{d\hat{\mathbf{z}}^{y}_{t}}{dt}=f_{\theta}(t,\mathbf{z}^{x}),\quad\text{with initial value }\hat{\mathbf{z}}^{y}_{0}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{I})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mi>y</mi></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>x</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>with initial value </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn><mi>y</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d\hat{\mathbf{z}}^{y}_{t}}{dt}=f_{\theta}(t,\mathbf{z}^{x}),\quad\text{with initial value }\hat{\mathbf{z}}^{y}_{0}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{I}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">By integrating from <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1</annotation></semantics></math> using a numerical ODE solver (e.g., Euler method), we can obtain the final prediction <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{z}}^{y}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><mi>y</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{z}}^{y}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Reviews of Related Generative and Editing Models</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The fields of image generation and image editing have always been complementary, and they have undergone several paradigm shifts. The first major breakthrough was the Generative Adversarial Network (GAN) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goodfellow et al., 2014</a>)</cite>, which introduced a novel adversarial training process. Then, key advancements in this era include architectural refinements like DCGAN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al., 2016</a>)</cite>, the development of conditional and text-to-image GANs such as the StackGAN series <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, AttnGAN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, and Cross-Modal Contrastive Learning based models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. The StyleGAN series <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karras et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karras et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karras et al., 2021</a>)</cite> marked a high point for GANs, achieving unprecedented photorealistic high-resolution image synthesis and offering fine-grained control over visual attributes through a disentangled latent space, which became a cornerstone for many subsequent editing techniques.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">More recently, the field has transitioned to Denoising Diffusion Models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, which have become the state-of-the-art for their superior image quality and textual coherence. A series of influential diffusion-based methods were introduced, including GLIDE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nichol et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, DALL<math id="A5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdot</annotation></semantics></math>E <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ramesh et al., 2021</a>)</cite> and its successor DALL<math id="A5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdot</annotation></semantics></math>E 2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ramesh et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, Imagen <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Saharia et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, and PIXART-<math id="A5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. The open-source Stable Diffusion (SD) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rombach et al., 2022</a>)</cite> model, trained on the large-scale LAION-5B dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schuhmann et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, further democratized high-quality image generation and quickly became a community standard.
A growing body of evidence suggests that Diffusion Transformers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peebles &amp; Xie, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Labs, 2024</a>)</cite> outperform U-Nets, motivating the shift toward training modern diffusion models with Transformer architectures.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Building on these powerful generative foundations, the domain of image editing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lan et al., 2025</a>)</cite> (generalized editing) has also advanced rapidly. Early diffusion-based methods like SDEdit <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meng et al., 2022</a>)</cite> demonstrated that real images could be edited by adding noise and then denoising with a new text prompt. A significant leap was made with instruction-guided editing, pioneered by InstructPix2Pix <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brooks et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, which enabled edits based on natural language commands. The field has since diversified with numerous innovative approaches. For instance, DragGAN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pan et al., 2023</a>)</cite> introduced a novel point-based interaction, allowing users to “drag” pixels to precisely deform object shapes. OmniControl <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tan et al., 2025</a>)</cite> further enhances controllability by creating a unified framework that accepts diverse spatial guidance signals for both synthesis and editing. This trend towards more powerful and versatile models is also reflected in large-scale systems like UniWorld <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, which uses a unified transformer for multi-modal understanding and generation, Step1X-Edit <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, fine-tuned from the FLUX architecture for superior instruction following, and multi-modal editors like Qwen-Image <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, which leverage Large Language Models (LLMs) to build more comprehensive visual editing frameworks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Addition Experiments Results</h2>

